  "worked_solution": "The function $F(t)=f(t)\\cdot g(t)$ is differentiable over the common domain where both $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable. A vector function is differentiable if and only if each component is differentiable.\n\nLet's now find the intervals where each of the functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are differentiable.\n\nFor the function $f(t):$ \n\n- The first  component $\\sqrt[3]{t-2}$ is differentiable on $(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second component, ${2}^{t+1}$ is differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\n- The third component $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t+1)$ is differentiable on $(-1,\\infty ).$\n\nSo,  the intersection of the domains of all three components of $f(t)$ is $(-1,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$  \n\nFor the function $g(t):$ \n\n- The first component $\\sqrt{t-1}$ is differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The second and third components, ${t}^{2}+1$ and ${t}^{4},$ respectively, are differentiable for all values of $t.$\n\nHence,  the intersection of the domains of all three components of $g(t)$ is  $(1,\\infty ).$    \n\nFinally, the common domain of differentiability for $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ is \n\n$$\n[(-1,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )]\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$",
